        ["PersonalModelInfoTitle"] = "How personal AI recommendations work", ["PersonalModelInfoShow"] = "How AI recommendations work", ["PersonalModelInfoTooltip"] = "Opens a separate window explaining what the local recommendations learn from and how they affect the audit.",
        ["PersonalModelInfoText"] = "Personal AI recommendations do not reopen files or learn directly from their contents. For each rated finding, they use only information PrivacyAudit has already calculated during the normal audit: Exposure Score, file size and age, extension, file/directory/scanner categories, counts of personal-data, secret and identity matches, credential-config, archive and document indicators, and—for media—faces, GPS, EXIF fields and camera serial data. A rated application-history object additionally contributes only local Jump List metadata already parsed by PrivacyAudit: application category, Automatic/Custom source, target availability, pinned state, interaction count and age, network-path status, and whether it matches an audit finding. Each feature snapshot is paired only with your 👍 or 👎; unrated objects are not training data. Ratings, feature snapshots and recommendation weights remain in your local PrivacyAudit profile.\n\nLearning is allowed only after at least 20 ratings, including at least 5 of each kind, with the smaller class representing at least 20% of all ratings. The app converts categories into numeric indicators, then a lightweight local classifier learns which combinations more often accompany your 👍 or 👎. It assigns each finding or remembered object a separate AI-priority score from 0 to 100. Applications are ranked by the average of their three strongest object recommendations; an application itself is never used as one broad training label. Recommendations are refreshed after 10 new ratings or on request; an incompatible feature schema is not used and must be learned again.\n\nFor example, if you usually rate old configuration or text findings with personal-data and secret indicators as interesting, another text finding with a similar set of calculated indicators may receive a high AI-priority score. If you tend to rate media with GPS, EXIF, faces or document indicators as interesting, those images may rise in AI-priority sorting. If you rate pinned, frequently used or missing historical paths as interesting, similar remembered objects may rise in Application history. This is only an extra recommendation: it does not change scanning, trigger extra file reads, or replace Exposure, Personal Data, Secrets or Archive scores.",
